UK Expenditure-Side GDP Model
Professional UK Macroeconomic Analysis, Econometrics and Scenario Forecasting

A Python-based macroeconomic modelling framework for analysing UK economic activity through the expenditure approach to GDP.

The project combines national-accounting identities, macroeconomic indicators, econometric analysis, scenario forecasting, interactive dashboards and automated economist-style reporting into a single reproducible analytical workflow.

Project Overview

The expenditure approach expresses GDP as:

[
Y = C + I + G + (X-M)
]

Where:

Y = Gross Domestic Product
C = Household consumption
I = Investment
G = Government expenditure
X = Exports
M = Imports
NX = Net exports (X - M)

The model examines how changes in domestic demand, investment, government activity and the external sector contribute to overall economic growth.

The analytical pipeline is:

RAW DATA
    ↓
DATA QUALITY CONTROL
    ↓
DATA CLEANING
    ↓
GDP EXPENDITURE IDENTITY
    ↓
MACROECONOMIC INDICATORS
    ↓
ECONOMETRICS
    ↓
SCENARIO FORECASTING
    ↓
RISK DASHBOARD
    ↓
ECONOMIST REPORT
Key Features
1. Data Quality Control

The project automatically checks:

Required variables
Missing observations
Duplicate observations
Year consistency
Numeric data types
Negative values
Dataset structure
GDP identity consistency
2. GDP Expenditure Identity

The model calculates:

Net Exports = Exports - Imports

Domestic Demand = Consumption + Investment + Government

GDP Expenditure = C + I + G + NX

It also calculates:

GDP identity residual
Absolute residual
Percentage error
Identity status
GDP growth
Component growth
Growth contributions
Business-cycle classifications

The model recognises that official national accounts can contain statistical discrepancies and that chain-volume measures may not always add perfectly.

